n"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Double"},{"type":"text","text":": counts pass "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int"},{"type":"text","text":" quickly (K₂₅ has 25²³). The Laplacian with one vertex removed"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"is eliminated densely as Grassmann, Taksar and Heyman eliminate (each pivot a sum of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"conductances, never a difference), so the count keeps a relative accuracy of about n · 10⁻¹⁶"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"whatever the weights; its pivots are multiplied with the binary exponent kept apart, so it"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"overflows only past "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Double"},{"type":"text","text":"’s range, to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".infinity"},{"type":"text","text":". When every weight is an integer"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"(unweighted included) and the count is below 2¹⁰⁰, it is computed exactly instead (two"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"determinants modulo 62-bit primes, combined by the Chinese remainder theorem) and rounded"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"once, so a count below 2⁵³ is exact. O(n³) time (less on sparse graphs), n² "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Double"},{"type":"text","text":"s."}]}],"kind":"content"}],"sections":[],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/SpectralGraphTheory\/documentation\/SpectralGraphTheory\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/numberOfSpanningTrees()","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/spectralgraphtheory\/graphprotocols\/graph\/numberofspanningtrees()"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"kind":"symbol","schemaVersion":{"major":0,"patch":0,"minor":3},"references":{"doc://SpectralGraphTheory/documentation/SpectralGraphTheory/GraphProtocols/Graph/numberOfSpanningTrees()":{"abstract":[{"text":"The number of spanning tr